# build output
build/

# runtime artifacts the app writes beside itself during development —
# never committed (the repo root is a public artifact; CLAUDE.md rule 2)
demo-org.json
demo-org.db
assets/
tiles/
preview-*.html
site/
*.miga
imgui.ini
exports/
templates/
documents/
databases/
backups/

# holiday credentials — NEVER committed. imgbb.key is plaintext (dev only);
# org.miga is the encrypted vault (still never committed — it's org data)
imgbb.key
*.miga

# demo-assets/ is the EXCEPTION: the shipped Cat Colony photos (fictional cats
# from cataas.com) — committed on purpose so the default dataset ships with the
# app. The runtime assets/ above stays ignored (user/real-org images).
!demo-assets/
!demo-assets/**

# temp-assets/ — the scratch drop for source material under examination (the
# Springfield policy PDF, sample transcripts). Public documents today, but the
# next thing dropped here might not be, and rule 2 says the repo root is a
# public artifact. Ignored by default so that is never a judgement call.
temp-assets/

# ── SYNC: this device's identity, and the merge scratch file (2026-08-27) ────
#
# `<database>.peerkey` holds an X25519 keypair — the SECRET half included. It is
# how another device recognises this one, and it is exactly what the short
# authentication string exists to protect. It never travels: not over the wire,
# not in the `.miga`, and above all not into a repo that is public from day one
# (ground rule 2). A leaked device key does not decrypt past traffic, but it
# does let someone else present as this device on a LAN, which is the whole
# thing the pairing code is arranged to make visible.
#
# `*.merging` is the write-temp file a merge renames into place. It only exists
# if a merge was interrupted between write and rename, and if one is sitting
# here it contains a copy of the organization's database.
*.peerkey
*.merging

# ── RUNTIME SIDECARS THE PROBE CAUGHT (2026-08-31) ──────────────────────────
#
# `demo-org.json` was ignored; the files the app writes BESIDE it were not, so
# a dry-run `git add -An` still staged the session log and the advisory lock.
# The lock carries an actor token and the log carries absolute local paths —
# neither is secret, both are noise, and neither is anybody's business.
demo-org.json.*
*.lock
*.migameta

# CTest's scratch directory (`Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log`), written into the
# source tree whenever ctest runs from the repo root rather than from build/.
Testing/

# Cloudflare Wrangler's local state, if a deploy was rehearsed from here.
.wrangler/

# ── VENDORED LIBSODIUM: the two files that carry the BUILDER's paths ─────────
#
# `libsodium.la` and `libsodium.pc` are libtool/pkg-config metadata emitted by
# whoever compiled the library, and both hard-code an absolute prefix — here,
# /c/Users/<name>/Documents/Projects/VoidHormiga/vendor/... . Nothing in
# CMakeLists.txt reads either file; we link `lib/libsodium.a` directly. They are
# dead weight that publishes the author's home directory, so they stay local.
vendor/libsodium/lib/libsodium.la
vendor/libsodium/lib/pkgconfig/

# ── AGENT CONFIG: personal, not project (2026-09-01) ────────────────────────
#
# `.claude/settings.json` here is 292 lines of THIS machine's permission
# allowlist, 125 of them naming absolute paths under the author's home
# directory and one-off test invocations. It is workflow, not project: it tells
# a stranger nothing about Void Hormiga and everything about one laptop.
#
# If a curated, path-free agent config is ever worth sharing, it can be added
# deliberately with `git add -f .claude/settings.json`. The default is out.
.claude/
# ── DEPLOY CREDENTIALS AND THE OPERATOR'S OWN STYLESHEET (2026-09-02) ────────
#
# A deploy token is a credential that can publish a website. The guide's own
# examples name `cf.key` and `gh.token` and say "gitignored, beside the
# database", so these patterns make that sentence true for anyone who follows
# it literally while working inside a checkout. Ground rule 2: the repo root is
# a public artifact.
#
# The `.deploy-curl.cfg` / `.rollback-curl.cfg` / `.cf-*` / `.gh-*` files hold a
# bearer header and are deleted when the call returns — but a crashed or killed
# publish leaves one behind, and a file that only exists after something went
# wrong is exactly the file nobody remembers to check.
#
# `custom.css` is not a secret; it is the operator's own stylesheet, staged from
# beside the database (render/assets.cpp). It belongs to whoever's site it is,
# not to this repository, and one committed here would silently restyle a
# developer's test renders.
*.token
*.key
!vendor/**/*.key
.deploy-curl.cfg
.rollback-curl.cfg
.cf-curl.cfg
.cf-body.json
.gh-curl.cfg
.gh-body.json
custom.css

# ── MESSAGES AND REPORTS BETWEEN PROJECTS (2026-09-15) ───────────────────────
#
# CLAUDE.md rule 4: needs and gaps are drafted at the repo root as
# MESSAGE_FOR_<RECIPIENT>_... for the author to relay; replies arrive as
# MESSAGE_FOR_VOIDHORMIGA_..., and field reports from other deployments as
# REPORT_FROM_<SENDER>_.... They are correspondence rather than project: a
# consumed one is folded into okf/log.md and deleted, and one still in flight can
# quote a client's site, their data, or a sibling project's unreleased work. The
# author asked for them to be ignored; the two that had been committed were
# removed from the index the same day (they stay on disk).
MESSAGE_FOR_*.md
REPORT_FROM_*.md

# ── ANDROID SIGNING (2026-09-24) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The release key that signs every Hormiga APK, and its password. Android
# accepts an update only when it is signed by the SAME key, so losing these
# means every phone must uninstall to update; committing them means anyone can
# ship an "update". They live beside android/build_apk.ps1, never in git, and
# are backed up by the author. See android/README.md.
android/*.keystore
android/*.keystore.pass
*.apk
*.apk.idsig
